{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 99,
    "dDurationMs": 2161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How far can you follow a compass needle?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2260,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As far as the north magnetic pole, where the needle starts spinning wildly?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7080,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compass needles align with magnetic field\nlines, and on the precise spot of magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11830,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "north, those field lines are vertical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14850,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So just tilt your compass 90 degrees and you can continue your journey - either down to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19450,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the molten iron dynamo surrounding the Earth’s core, or up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23910,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But up to where?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25690,
    "dDurationMs": 6056,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer - to everywhere - and today, that’s where we’re going to go."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36341,
    "dDurationMs": 3029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you can see gravitational fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39370,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would mean seeing the literal threads of\nthe fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43610,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Threads tugged lightly towards the Earth,\ntightly towards the Sun, or into inescapable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48519,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knots towards black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50969,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s really only one gravitational\nfield in the universe - manifest as the fabric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55550,
    "dDurationMs": 1189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56739,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it governs the formation of every major\nstructure in the universe, from the smallest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61100,
    "dDurationMs": 2529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moon to the largest cluster of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63629,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for all its importance, gravity is a bit\nboring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68159,
    "dDurationMs": 3991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universal gravitation field is just a\ngrid with some dips in it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72150,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Personally, I’d rather be able to see magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76590,
    "dDurationMs": 4709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetism is one half of electromagnetism, one of the four fundamental forces alongside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81299,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82299,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetism shares a property with gravity that none of the other forces have - it manifests"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87000,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on enormous scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89700,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The nuclear forces are short range, and the\nelectrostatic force never adds up to much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94039,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because its positive and negative charges\ntend to cancel it out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97789,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not magnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99219,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s generated when electrically charged\nparticles move."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103179,
    "dDurationMs": 4201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if the substance is electrically neutral\nyou’ll still get a magnetic field as long"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107380,
    "dDurationMs": 3699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the charges are moving in opposite directions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111079,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means magnetic fields can add up - and magnetism adds up to having enormous influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117619,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the development of structure in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121939,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Understanding magnetic fields is of fundamental importance to astrophysicists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126069,
    "dDurationMs": 2771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so we should probably understand them too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128840,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, let’s review how magnetism works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132060,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic field lines form in concentric circles around moving charges."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136400,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that charge moves in a loop, that results in a dipole field - sort of a torus around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142030,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the loop with the field threading the loop\nand shooting off at the poles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146320,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So those are magnetic field lines - but what\ndo they do?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150380,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, a moving charged particle will feel\na force perpendicular to both its direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155300,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of motion and to the field lines - and the net result of that is that charged particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160210,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tend to spiral around magnetic field lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163730,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if that charge already has a circular\nmotion - for example the electric current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in an electromagnet, or the aligned electron\nspins in a ferromagnet, then that current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173640,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will want to loop around the magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176420,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the circular current produces its own\ndipole field, and the net result is that dipole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181410,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields always try to align themselves with\nother dipole fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185520,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that, by the way, is how compasses work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189030,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of compasses - a minute ago we were standing at the north pole with a compass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193100,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needle pointing straight up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195250,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where exactly does that field line go?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197970,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As with the gravitational field, in a sense\nthere’s only one universal magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203110,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So while most of the Earth’s dipole field\nloops back - but some of those field lines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208670,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connect to this greater magnetic field of\nthe solar system - and even of the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214480,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s hitch a ride on a field line and\nsee how far it takes us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218610,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I don’t want to spend too much time\nin the solar system - greater magnetic wonders"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223260,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lie beyond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224260,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But while we’re here, it’s worth following\none of Earth’s field lines that connects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227890,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directly to the surface of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230600,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a violent place, magnetically speaking."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233720,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here the field is generated by electrical\ncurrents flowing in the searing plasma near"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237950,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun’s surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239710,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Earth’s solid inner core and mantle\nregulate the flow in its liquid outer core,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244980,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resulting in a clean dipole field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245980,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Sun is entirely fluid, and its rate\nof rotation speeds up towards the equator."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252910,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the dipole field gets twisted up\nover time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256160,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic field lines cross each other, and\nenormous magnetic energy densities pile up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261470,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see those tangled field lines in ultraviolet light as charged particles spiral along them,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267010,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up and down from the Sun’s surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269310,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the pressure gets too high, these field lines snap and then reconnect, and in the process spray"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274840,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that magnetic field out into the solar system - carrying high energy particles with them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279980,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These coronal mass ejections join the solar wind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283230,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Follow one of these magnetic blasts and you’ll spiral through the solar system on a giangantic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288260,
    "dDurationMs": 1270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic tornado."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289530,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is still the Sun’s magnetic field,\nwhich connects here and there to the piddling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293350,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little fields of the planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295460,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "About 4x the distance to Pluto, the Sun’s magnetic field connects to the field of the galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300410,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301410,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or smashes into it, depending on how you look at it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303870,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the heliopause, the boundary of the\nheliosphere, which defines the limit of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309280,
    "dDurationMs": 2350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun’s influence in the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311630,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although it’s less of a sphere and more\nof a teardrop - dragged into that shape by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316130,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun’s orbital motion through the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318830,
    "dDurationMs": 1270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do we know that?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320100,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, we have sent compasses that far - or\nrather magnetometers - on board the Voyager"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325190,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1 and 2 spacecraft which crossed the boundary into interstellar space a few years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330220,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those didn’t reveal the shape of the\nheliosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334130,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was measured for the first time only\nlast year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336750,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA’s IBEX mission, which used a sort of\nsolar wind sonar - it mapped how bursts of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342820,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar wind material were reflected back towards the Earth from the edge of the heliosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346860,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beyond the heliosphere, seeing magnetic fields gets trickier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351960,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately astronomers are also tricky, and\nso have tricks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356650,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that the Milky Way is full of\nnatural compasses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360870,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The interstellar medium - the space between the stars - is scattered with tiny specks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365360,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of dust produced in past supernova explosions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368630,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These specks tend to align with the local\nmagnetic field of the Galaxy in exactly the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373280,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same way as our iron filings align around a bar magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377710,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When light passes through the dusty interstellar medium it gets scattered by these grains - it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383220,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bounces off them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384850,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the pattern of alignment of these grains\nimprints a pattern on the scattered light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389920,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The light gets polarized - which means the\ndirection of its electric and magnetic fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394950,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pick up a preferred direction rather than\nbeing random."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397910,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By measuring this polarization we can map\nthe direction of these tiny compass needles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402260,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so map the magnetic field of the Milky\nWay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405190,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has now been done in incredible detail by the Planck mission, which mapped polarization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411340,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the cosmic microwave background - the ubiquitous radiation left over from the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416180,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The resulting map reveals a whirlwind of magnetism tangled through the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421720,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "KInda makes me wonder if van Gogh could see magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually there’s a more traditional way\nto map the magnetic fields of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429720,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These fields drive the motion of lone electrons throughout the interstellar medium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435370,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When radio waves interact with those electrons, their polarizations are also affected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439919,
    "dDurationMs": 2021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a bit different though."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441940,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The presence of these electrons tends to slow light down - just as light is slowed down in air"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446870,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or glass - but to a much smaller degree."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449330,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that slowing depends on the polarization of the light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452410,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case, it depends on the circular polarization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455430,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the electric and magnetic fields of a collection of photons all tend to point in the same direction,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460540,
    "dDurationMs": 2650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we say the light is linearly polarized."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463190,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if those fields are not fixed but rather\nrotate in the same direction, we say the light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468260,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is circularly polarized - and it can be left-\nor right-polarized, meaning clockwise or counterclockwise"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474560,
    "dDurationMs": 1230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475790,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The electrons in their magnetic fields tend\nto slow one circular polarization direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481050,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482440,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The net result of this is that the linear\npolarization - which is sort of the sum of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486470,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the circular polarizations - gets rotated\nin an effect called Faraday rotation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492800,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So by measuring the Faraday rotation of distant radio sources we can also map magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498710,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has been done for the Milky Way using the light from pulsars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501650,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We even have clear views of magnetic fields in many distant spiral galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506740,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see that the field tends to be threaded\nalong the spiral arms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510840,
    "dDurationMs": 4759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the densest regions of those galactic disks - places where magnetic fields have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515599,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confined the charged particles of the interstellar plasma."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519879,
    "dDurationMs": 4641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that plasma in turn drags the magnetic\nfields in orbit around the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524520,
    "dDurationMs": 2629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so galaxies have magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527149,
    "dDurationMs": 1961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But where do those magnetic fields come from?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529110,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a surprisingly open question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531790,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Large-scale magnetic fields can grow and reinforce themselves in very particular configurations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537059,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called dynamos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538059,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Earth’s dynamo, swirls of magma are induced by the coriolis force, and while these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543649,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are initially turbulent, they arrange themselves into a series of self-supporting flows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550189,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These amplify what starts out as a very weak and disordered field into the ordered and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555230,
    "dDurationMs": 2459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powerful field that surrounds the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557689,
    "dDurationMs": 3731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We did a whole episode on this cool effect\nif you want to learn more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561420,
    "dDurationMs": 6079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The exact process for the Milky Way isn’t\nas well understood, but the ingredients for a dynamo are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567499,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all there: we have differential rotation in\nthe disk which can lead to coriolis-induced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572839,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helical flows, and those flows can also be\nproduced by supernova explosions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578279,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those supernovae may also give us the seeds of magnetic fields that can then be amplified"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583110,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the galactic dynamo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585600,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it got there, the Milky Way has built itself a substantial magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590100,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that field helps build the Milky Way in\nreturn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593350,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic fields generated by collapsing gas clouds help to slow the rotation of those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597810,
    "dDurationMs": 2269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clouds - expel angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600079,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without that, those clouds would never be able to collapse all the way into stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605509,
    "dDurationMs": 4211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And magnetic fields also facilitate star formation after stars die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609720,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic blasts accompany every supernova explosion, and these help to compress gas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614170,
    "dDurationMs": 3649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the path of that explosion, triggering\nbursts of new star formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617819,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those same supernovae are expected to blast their own guts entirely out of the galaxy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623379,
    "dDurationMs": 3751,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which should result in all those newly-formed elements being lost to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627130,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the galactic magnetic field constrains\nthat flow, funneling some of it into vast"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631589,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galactic fountains erupting from the poles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634139,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s right - if you follow a magnetic\nfield line too far, you may accidentally leave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638449,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639959,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually, these galactic fountains are\nincredibly important for building galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644550,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their material tends to spill into the space\naround the galaxy before slowly raining back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649800,
    "dDurationMs": 3409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in, where it can be used for new star formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653209,
    "dDurationMs": 5701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other cool thing that galactic magnetic\nfields do is that they act as colossal particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658910,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerators."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659910,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you thought the 27 km ring of the large\nhadron collider was big, try the 300,000 light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665340,
    "dDurationMs": 2789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year ring around the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668129,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons and atomic nuclei can be accelerated in this magnetic field to high energies - into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672769,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we call cosmic rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675379,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These can also be accelerated in the magnetic shock-fronts of supernova explosions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680089,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the most energetic cosmic rays are accelerated by the strongest magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685589,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those occur deep in galactic cores near the\ngigantic black hole that dwells there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690329,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that black hole is feeding and surrounded\nby a disk of gas, we have what is known as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694959,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an active galactic nucleus - the most powerful of which are called quasars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700129,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Intense magnetic fields live just above the event horizon of some of thses black hole, and thread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705499,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the infalling disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707389,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those fields grab particles of matter and\naccelerate them to incredible energies, flinging"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712369,
    "dDurationMs": 1671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic rays out into the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714040,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve even taken our first picture of the\nsuch a magnetic field - in the polarized light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719350,
    "dDurationMs": 6229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding the M81 supermassive black hole observed by the event horizon telescope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725579,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s an even more spectacular result of these magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729319,
    "dDurationMs": 5651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thick flows of gas can be catapulted through the surrounding galaxy in powerful jets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734970,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In some cases, these jets puncture the galaxy and plume out in radio lobes which can dwarf"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740550,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire galaxy that spawned them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743660,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These jets carry magnetic fields out into\nthe cosmos, and we see them through the radio"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748290,
    "dDurationMs": 4289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light emitted by electrons that spiral slowly in these vast structures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752579,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, we’ve ridden our magnetic field lines\npretty far and into some strange places."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758009,
    "dDurationMs": 6411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To summarize what we’ve learned: magnetic fields are ubiquitous, powerful, and extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764420,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complicated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765420,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In some ways they’re the curse of the astrophysicist because they’re so tricky to model - far"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770939,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more so than simple-old gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773579,
    "dDurationMs": 4052,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it turns out that without cosmic-scale\nmagnetic fields we probably wouldn’t be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777631,
    "dDurationMs": 1548,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779179,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As such, astrophysicists are spending more\nand more time learning how to map and to model"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784309,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic fields, to better understand the\nmysteries of this magnetic space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790459,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As ever, we want to express our eternal gratitude to all our Patreon supporters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795149,
    "dDurationMs": 4551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your enthusiasm is what keeps us motivated to keep doing this, and your Patreon support"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799700,
    "dDurationMs": 2879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a big part of HOW we can keep doing this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802579,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today, our extra extra special shoutout\ngoes to Stephen Wilcox who’s supporting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807059,
    "dDurationMs": 2231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us at the quasar level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809290,
    "dDurationMs": 4799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most people don’t know this, but Stephen\nis SO generous and amazing that scientists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814089,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have to factor in his personal magnetism when they calculate the Earth’s geomagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819129,
    "dDurationMs": 5031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The US air force weather agency sometimes gives aurora alerts when Stephen travels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824160,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stephen, thank you for your help."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827110,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re covering comments from our episode on the weirdness of quantum spin, and then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831920,
    "dDurationMs": 5569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our episode that tried to answer the question where exactly are the alternate worlds in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837489,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the many worlds interpretation of quantum\nmechanics?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839899,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although fair warning - this will mostly be\nabout many worlds - because there were, appropriately,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845459,
    "dDurationMs": 1351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many comments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846810,
    "dDurationMs": 1129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the spin episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847939,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Howard Smith asks how two permanent magnets can pull each other together?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852459,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do the bound currents do the work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855839,
    "dDurationMs": 4411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Howard, you just answered a question that’s vexed some of our greatest living philosophers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860250,
    "dDurationMs": 3769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- magnets, how the bleep do they work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864019,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moving charges exert forces on other moving charges - that’s what it always comes down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868990,
    "dDurationMs": 1789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to with magnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870779,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In ferromagnets, the “moving” charges\nare the electron spins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874709,
    "dDurationMs": 3771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Colm offers an important historical detail."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878480,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The anomalous Zeeman effect was discovered not by Zeeman but by an Irish Physicist, Thomas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883790,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Preston, and Preston never seems to get any credit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886290,
    "dDurationMs": 1959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seems a fair point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888249,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As an irish physicist myself - by descent, anyway - who has way more notoriety than he deserves,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894569,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I support noting much better physicists who have less than they deserve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900149,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the many worlds episode we asked where are those worlds - but a few of you brought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906379,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up an equally fascinating question -HOW are those worlds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910959,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wait, that wasn’t it - it was how many are those worlds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915519,
    "dDurationMs": 5021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example Zapp Brannigan asks what percentage of quantum interactions separate into worlds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920540,
    "dDurationMs": 2519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "versus recombining again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923059,
    "dDurationMs": 5167,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Jack Williams leads us on a thought experiment that demonstrates a potential absurdity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928226,
    "dDurationMs": 4824,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saying that for every measurement\nof a spin-1/2 state we get 2 worlds, but for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933050,
    "dDurationMs": 5499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every measurement of photon number we get countably infinite splits, and for particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938549,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position it’s uncountably infinite splits\nper measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941819,
    "dDurationMs": 5231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the answer of many worlds there are is that it depends what you mean by worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947050,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you mean every possible configuration of\nparticle properties in the universe - then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951410,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the answer is there are infinite worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954110,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A world for every infinitesimal difference\nin every particle property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958110,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, particle position wavefunction\nis typically a smooth distribution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962459,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of possible locations - some more probable\nthan others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965819,
    "dDurationMs": 4811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each infinitesimal location can be thought\nof as a “world” and some worlds have more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970630,
    "dDurationMs": 2959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weight - more probability than others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973589,
    "dDurationMs": 4881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But worlds in that sense aren’t separate,\ndistinguishable realities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978470,
    "dDurationMs": 6390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get this sort of splitting, two parts of that wavefunction need to influence other particles in ways"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984860,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are distinguishable from each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987470,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The number of distinguishable worlds depends on the rate at which distinct decoherence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992190,
    "dDurationMs": 4879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens - so far, far fewer than the number\nof quantum wiggles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997069,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But possibly still infinite, though that’s\nstill being debated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000439,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly, there’s a ton more to be said\nabout this so maybe I’ll save it for a proper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005379,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006379,
    "dDurationMs": 2661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A related point is regarding probability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009040,
    "dDurationMs": 8250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MrRogueTech and Master Piece point out that if many worlds gives one world per option,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017290,
    "dDurationMs": 4509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then what does that mean for the different\nweights - probabilities of those different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021799,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction branches?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023999,
    "dDurationMs": 5391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a really good point.- and one we’ll\nexplore in that half-promised episode I just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mentioned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030390,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The probabilities must be reflected in the\nnumber of worlds for each quantum outcome"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034620,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- because in the many worlds interpretation,\nan observer is more likely to see the most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039360,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probable outcomes simply because there are more worlds in which that outcome manifests."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045110,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the splitting only made one world per distinct outcome then indeed probability would be destroyed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051230,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see differences in likelihood, and therefore probability is not destroyed, so IF the many worlds explanation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058360,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is right then the number of worlds is weighted by the probability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062980,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One thing that will influence the number of\nworlds is if there’s any degree of suppression"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067990,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069580,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, do the weakest, less “probable” parts of the wavefunction get pruned?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075070,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Barefoot expresses this eloquently, drawing\non the analogy of ripples on a pond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080539,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those ripples don’t really add up indefinitely\n- there’s a damping function which suppresses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085789,
    "dDurationMs": 2951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tallest peaks and deepest troughs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088740,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So Barefoot asks what if there’s a damping function that suppresses the universal wavefunction?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095230,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That could massively cut down the number of worlds to only the most probable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100240,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Barefoot also point out that this divide isn’t needed for quantum mechanics to work - its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106600,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps only helpful in making us less freaked out by the idea of a stupid number of parallel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112090,
    "dDurationMs": 1449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113539,
    "dDurationMs": 4961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And anyway, as many of you pointed out - we don’t really need wavefunction damping if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118500,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have the time variance authority pruning\nworlds for us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122370,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Really, that entire series can be framed as\nan alternative interpretation of quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  } ]
}
